ion arithmetic and covers all the way up through the algebra of linear equations. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations II, the second step of the journey towards university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1009 "Solidify your arithmetic, learn about variables and graphs, level up your algebra, and learn the essentials of geometry." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1012 113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1013 "MF1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "042e624c-a46d-509f-a48e-61bd6d3e082c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1031 "The Factors of a Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1033 3942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048351 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "97ce0d6d-e16e-5428-a47c-ae7808354f60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1031 "Statements and Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1033 58 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049002 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d2783ca6-3cd7-54fc-baaa-eb47c3d52ff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1031 "Prime Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1033 829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049086 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d960bb81-472b-5bc1-a127-bf03582ff4d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1031 "Prime and Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1033 840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050915 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "70dfa99f-c825-5da5-8721-f8fa26795d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087420 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087420 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a list containing only prime numbers?\n\nI. $3, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 17$\n\nII. $3, \\: 7, \\: 11, \\: 29$\n\nIII. $2, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 13$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "580e631f-10aa-5f3c-92a9-592c879df478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087420 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in each list.\n\n- List I includes $8$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors:  $1,$ $2,$ $4,$ and $8.$\n\n- List II contains only prime numbers since $3,$ $7,$ $11,$ and $29$ have only two factors each: $1$ and themselves.\n\n- List III contains $4$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1,$ $2,$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087421 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087421 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lists contains only composite numbers?\n\nI. $4, \\: 16, \\: 24, \\: 28$\n\nII. $6, \\: 15, \\: 19, \\: 20$\n\nIII. $8, \\: 15, \\: 21, \\: 29$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1e2d3d3-8819-5988-8ec1-195cbd548773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087421 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each list.\n\n- List I contains only composite numbers. Notice that all of them have $2$ as a factor.\n\n- List II includes $19,$ which is prime.\n\n- List III contains $29,$ which is prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087422 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087422 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are prime?\n\n$$\n13, \\quad 15, \\quad  17, \\quad  1, \\quad  6\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72d7834c-fdfd-5f05-ad5f-cd9f6a2fcffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087422 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13.$\n\n- $15$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $15\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n15= 3\\times 5\n$$\n\n- $17$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $17.$\n\n- $1$ is *not* prime since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* composite either.\n\n- $6$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $6\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n6= 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $13$ and $17$ are prime." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Prime Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "af9aa6af-5cbe-5933-9c7b-62e32a63386c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd997467-85ee-5288-a574-2719cbff167b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying One as Neither Prime Nor Composite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b45eb1fc-7e00-5ece-8dcf-f27e6d4fb78e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1045 "Prime and Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "577ff41d-cd6b-5bfb-9a19-db4815d18bb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1061 40187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61039992-084b-5a41-8a84-d5d32f71239e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1061 40188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dbf16eb4-9e74-5637-b88b-0c59bf07c9e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1061 40189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c44f455-2994-5b1f-b5a9-5ea9a33d7a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1061 40192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b769077a-6aa9-51b2-b700-b41bc8260115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1061 40196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03211f8f-c13f-570c-b8d1-d99bafd6d977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1061 40237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "758bd934-4f53-5080-bca6-e617305ccced" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder. \n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1.$\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1065 6639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee084b63-b5c9-54fe-8f73-750445535efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1064 "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**. \n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2,$ and $4.$ \n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$ \n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1065 6640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db98b259-0f36-53b5-b62a-788af51d2b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1067 "Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1064 "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both. \n\nThe only exception is the number $1.$ The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1065 6646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2092df40-d7b5-5553-a2d1-05d8166f06bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1067 "One Is Neither Prime Nor Composite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434614 #edb/ref 1080 "$13$ and $23$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434614 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434614 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0170376b-1703-5848-91b7-1bdf5372127a" #edb/