d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434650 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $40$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $10$ is a factor of $40:$ \n\n$$\n40=10\\times 4\n$$\n\n- $1$ is *not* prime since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* composite either.\n\n- $23$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $23.$\n\n- $36$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $6$ is a factor of $36:$ \n\n$$\n36=6\\times 6\n$$\n\n- $19$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $19.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$19$ and $23$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are composite?\n\n$$\n4,\\,15,\\,5,\\,17,\\,21\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28d07878-4721-5eb5-b0be-c107be86a38a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434651 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the numbers in turn.\n\n- $4$ is composite as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $2$ is a factor of $4:$ \n\n$$\n4=2\\times 2\n$$\n\n- $15$ is composite as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $15:$ \n\n$$\n15=3\\times 5\n$$\n\n- $5$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $5.$\n\n- $17$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $17.$\n\n- $21$ is composite as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $21:$ \n\n$$\n21=3\\times 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$4,$ $15,$ and $21$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are composite?\n\n$$\n14,\\,39,\\,1,\\,19,\\,2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5be044-29e6-5f84-b096-b5932b361863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434652 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $14$ is composite as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $2$ is a factor of $14:$ \n\n$$\n14=2\\times 7\n$$\n\n- $39$ is composite as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $39:$ \n\n$$\n39=3\\times 13\n$$\n\n- $1$ is *not* composite since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* prime either.\n\n- $19$ is *not* composite since it has only two factors: $1$ and $19.$\n\n- $2$ is *not* composite since it has only two factors: $1$ and $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$14$ and $39$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are **not** prime?\n\n$$\n12,\\,13,\\,19,\\,23,\\,29\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "972da6da-62d7-5b9f-b8a6-856a5ad278f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434653 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the numbers in turn.\n\n- $12$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $2$ is a factor of $12:$ \n\n$$\n12=2\\times 6\n$$\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13.$\n\n- $19$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $19.$\n\n- $23$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $23.$\n\n- $29$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $29.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$12$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers is prime?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6642a8e-2349-55aa-9e5d-871dc8226b24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434654 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$\n\nThe only number that satisfies this definition is $7.$ It has only two factors: $1$ and $7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =1\\times 7 \\\\  & =7\\times 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll the other given numbers have at least one factor distinct from itself, and $1:$\n\n- $21$ has $3$ and $7$ as factors:\n\n$$\n21=3\\times 7\n$$\n\n- $9$ has $3$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n9=3\\times 3\n$$\n\n- $24$ has $3$ and $8$ as factors:\n\n$$\n24=3\\times 8\n$$\n\n- $15$ has $3$ and $5$ as factors:\n\n$$\n15=3\\times 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers is prime?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5bec05f-8e68-5a1d-a365-731ec8194eb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434655 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$\n\nThe only number that satisfies this definition is $3.$ It has only two factors: $1$ and $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =1\\times 3 \\\\  & =3\\times 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll the other given numbers have at least one factor distinct from itself, and $1:$\n\n- $9$ has $3$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n9=3\\times 3\n$$\n\n- $27$ has $3$ and $9$ as factors:\n\n$$\n27=3\\times 9\n$$\n\n- $8$ has $2$ and $4$ as factors:\n\n$$\n8=2\\times 4\n$$\n\n- $32$ has $2$ and $16$ as factors:\n\n$$\n32=2\\times 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
